WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.010
Picture a café owner checking her balance on her phone.

2
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:10.223
A minute ago, she moved 30.00 out of her account, and the app said OK.

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.634
So the balance should be 70.00.

4
00:00:12.634 --> 00:00:14.631
Her phone still shows 100.00.

5
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:18.523
The app said OK, and the app is showing the old number.

6
00:00:18.523 --> 00:00:20.450
So what did OK actually mean?

7
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:21.713
Hold that question.

8
00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:27.170
Behind the app, each balance is kept on three computers, so one can break safely.

9
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:30.575
With one computer in charge, you'd just ask that one.

10
00:00:30.575 --> 00:00:32.952
Here, none of the three is in charge.

11
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.496
Whichever computer gets a request passes it on to all three copies.

12
00:00:37.496 --> 00:00:40.125
Let's call that computer the coordinator.

13
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:42.457
Let's leave computers for a moment.

14
00:00:42.457 --> 00:00:47.356
Picture three clerks in three towns, each keeping a copy of the same account book.

15
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:53.541
You phone each new entry to all three, and each clerk writes down the amount and the time of your call.

16
00:00:53.541 --> 00:00:57.097
The question is: how many must say written before you hang up?

17
00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:58.188
Let's map it.

18
00:00:58.188 --> 00:01:01.343
Each clerk is a copy, which is called a replica.

19
00:01:01.343 --> 00:01:04.170
You, making the calls, are the coordinator.

20
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:09.627
And every entry carries its time, so when two books disagree, the newer entry wins.

21
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.973
First idea: wait for all three.

22
00:01:11.973 --> 00:01:16.655
Then every book has every entry, and any clerk you ask later is right.

23
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.693
But every call is as slow as the slowest clerk.

24
00:01:19.693 --> 00:01:23.616
And if one clerk is out to lunch, you can't write anything at all.

25
00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:26.349
One sandwich, and the whole account is frozen.

26
00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:28.808
Second idea: wait for just one.

27
00:01:28.808 --> 00:01:31.194
Fast, and almost always possible.

28
00:01:31.194 --> 00:01:33.435
That's what the café's app did.

29
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:36.477
Clerk C was restarting when the call came.

30
00:01:36.477 --> 00:01:42.299
A and B wrote down 70.00 at time t2, and the app heard OK after the first one answered.

31
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:46.022
Then C comes back, still holding 100.00 from t1.

32
00:01:46.022 --> 00:01:49.365
And the phone's next read asks just one clerk.

33
00:01:49.365 --> 00:01:50.745
It happens to be C.

34
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.430
So OK meant: at least one clerk wrote it down, somewhere.

35
00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.527
Nothing promised that the read would ask that one.

36
00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:04.427
What we want is simple: the clerks a read asks should always include at least one who has the write.

37
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.928
Let's give the two sides names.

38
00:02:06.928 --> 00:02:09.811
W is how many clerks must confirm a write.

39
00:02:09.811 --> 00:02:12.076
R is how many clerks a read asks.

40
00:02:12.076 --> 00:02:14.341
N is the number of copies, three.

41
00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.730
Try W equals 2 and R equals 2.

42
00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.460
The write is on A and B.

43
00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:20.551
The read asks any two clerks.

44
00:02:20.551 --> 00:02:23.580
Can it pick two who both missed the write?

45
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:24.733
Pause and guess.

46
00:02:27.933 --> 00:02:29.654
Let's try every pair.

47
00:02:29.654 --> 00:02:31.457
A and B: both have it.

48
00:02:31.457 --> 00:02:32.931
A and C: A has it.

49
00:02:32.931 --> 00:02:34.406
B and C: B has it.

50
00:02:34.406 --> 00:02:37.929
Every pair includes a clerk with the write.

51
00:02:38.167 --> 00:02:39.862
Say it asks C and A.

52
00:02:39.862 --> 00:02:41.558
C says 100.00 at t1.

53
00:02:41.558 --> 00:02:43.168
A says 70.00 at t2.

54
00:02:43.168 --> 00:02:47.068
The newer one wins, so the read returns 70.00.

55
00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:52.445
And on its way out, the coordinator tells C the new entry, so C catches up too.

56
00:02:52.445 --> 00:02:54.149
That's called read repair.

57
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.146
Now, you might be thinking: so the fix is to read from two.

58
00:02:58.146 --> 00:02:59.860
Let's be fair to that idea.

59
00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.527
In the café, R equals 2 would have worked.

60
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.958
But only by luck.

61
00:03:03.958 --> 00:03:08.929
The write happened to reach two clerks, and W equals 1 promised only one.

62
00:03:08.929 --> 00:03:11.993
Run it again, with the write reaching only A.

63
00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:15.380
Now a read of B and C gets 100.00 from both.

64
00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.634
Both agree, both are old, and nothing says so.

65
00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:19.129
Well...

66
00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:22.384
that's two wrong answers in perfect agreement.

67
00:03:22.633 --> 00:03:25.193
So it's never R alone, or W alone.

68
00:03:25.193 --> 00:03:26.849
It's the two together.

69
00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:28.269
Here's the rule.

70
00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:34.016
If R plus W is more than N, every read meets every write the client heard OK for.

71
00:03:34.233 --> 00:03:39.069
Two plus two is four, and you can't fit four different clerks into three towns.

72
00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:40.967
At least one is in both groups.

73
00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:45.778
A group big enough to overlap every other such group is called a quorum.

74
00:03:45.778 --> 00:03:47.924
For three copies, a quorum is two.

75
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.680
For three copies, here are the choices.

76
00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.096
One and one is fast, but can be stale.

77
00:03:53.096 --> 00:03:55.195
Two and two is the common choice.

78
00:03:55.195 --> 00:03:58.183
One and three gives fast writes and slow reads.

79
00:03:58.183 --> 00:04:02.761
Three and one gives fast reads, and one absent clerk blocks every write.

80
00:04:02.933 --> 00:04:06.843
The same count decides what still works while clerks are away.

81
00:04:06.843 --> 00:04:09.807
With one of three away, two and two still work.

82
00:04:09.807 --> 00:04:14.537
With two away, both fail, and the coordinator refuses before it even tries.

83
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:20.301
Cassandra, a free database that spreads its data over many computers, works this way.

84
00:04:20.301 --> 00:04:25.380
Each request picks its own count, with a setting called the consistency level.

85
00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.878
ONE waits for one copy.

86
00:04:26.878 --> 00:04:28.831
QUORUM waits for two of three.

87
00:04:28.831 --> 00:04:30.459
ALL waits for every copy.

88
00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:31.997
And here's the catch.

89
00:04:31.997 --> 00:04:34.962
Out of the box, Cassandra's shell starts at ONE.

90
00:04:34.962 --> 00:04:37.248
So until you choose, you're the café.

91
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.949
Write and read at QUORUM, and you're back to two plus two.

92
00:04:41.233 --> 00:04:42.186
One more limit.

93
00:04:42.186 --> 00:04:44.471
Counting copies isn't a transaction.

94
00:04:44.471 --> 00:04:52.280
If two people write the same balance at the same moment, the later time wins, and the other write is gone without an error.

95
00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:53.740
That's the next lesson.

96
00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.669
So, what did OK mean?

97
00:04:55.669 --> 00:04:57.977
It meant W copies have the write.

98
00:04:57.977 --> 00:05:00.705
Whether your read sees it depends on R.

99
00:05:00.705 --> 00:05:04.552
Keep R plus W above N, and the two groups have to meet.

100
00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.173
One quick check.

101
00:05:06.173 --> 00:05:07.917
Writes wait for one clerk.

102
00:05:07.917 --> 00:05:12.075
How many clerks must a read ask, to be sure it sees the write?

103
00:05:12.075 --> 00:05:13.148
Pause and think.

104
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.048
All three.

105
00:05:17.048 --> 00:05:19.638
One plus three is four, more than three.

106
00:05:19.638 --> 00:05:23.524
Any fewer, and the read could miss the one clerk who has it.
